This  manual pages describes the IPsec protocol.  For the network
device driver please see is a security protocol in  the  Internet
Protocol  (IP) layer.  is defined for both IPv4 and IPv6 and con‐
sists of three sub‐protocols: protects IP payloads from wire‐tap‐
ping (interception) by encrypting them with secret key cryptogra‐
phy algorithms.  guarantees the integrity of IP packets and  pro‐
tects  them from intermediate alteration or impersonation, by at‐
taching cryptographic checksums computed by  one‐way  hash  func‐
tions.   increases  the  communication performance by compressing
the datagrams.  has two operation modes: is for protecting  peer‐
to‐peer  communication  between end nodes.  includes IP‐in‐IP en‐
capsulation operation and is designed for security  gateways,  as
in  Virtual  Private Network (VPN) configurations.  is controlled
by two engines in the kernel: one for key management and one  for
policy.   The key management engine can be accessed from userland
by using sockets.  The socket API is  defined  in  RFC2367.   The
policy  engine can be controlled through the API, operations, and
the interface.  The kernel implements an extended version of  the
interface  and  allows you to define IPsec policy like per‐packet
filters.  is used to define per‐socket behavior, and is  used  to
define host‐wide default behavior.  The kernel does not implement
dynamic encryption key exchange protocols like IKE That should be
done  in userland using the APIs described above.  The kernel im‐
plements experimental policy management code.  You can manage the
